  "en": {
    "title": "Clear Water Dream Meaning — Korean Dream Interpretation Guide",
    "metaDescription": "Discover what dreaming of clear water means in Korean dream interpretation. Covers drinking, swimming, washing, and overflowing clear water dreams — with cultural context, variations, and FAQ.",
    "h1": "Clear Water Dream Meaning — A Powerful Omen of Wealth and Wish Fulfillment",
    "intro": "In Korean dream interpretation, a clear water dream ranks among the most auspicious signs you can receive. Water so transparent the bottom is visible signals that blocked fortune is about to flow freely and long-held wishes are finally within reach. There is one important nuance to keep in mind, though — the clarity of the water alone does not tell the full story. The state of the water's surface can flip the interpretation entirely.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Clear Water Dreams — Auspicious Interpretations",
        "content": "Seeing clear, transparent water or swimming freely within it is read as a sign that wealth luck is rising significantly and business affairs are about to flourish. Water so pure the bottom is visible also indicates that both physical and mental well-being are reaching a stable, healthy state. Long-blocked endeavors will flow freely, and a helpful mentor figure is likely to appear and offer support — a recurring theme in Korean traditional dream lore.\n\nDrinking clear water in a dream is a particularly powerful omen. All long-standing worries and problems will be resolved, those who have been ill will recover swiftly, and anyone preparing for job applications or examinations can anticipate good news. The cleaner the water, the stronger the fortune boost. The satisfaction of quenching thirst in the dream amplifies the reading further.\n\nWashing one's face, hands, or feet in clear water foretells improved romantic fortune, the lifting of lingering worries, and the fulfillment of cherished wishes. It also symbolizes releasing past negativity — a fresh start washing away what no longer serves you. Doing laundry in a clear mountain stream carries similar positive associations, pointing toward business success and favorable employment outcomes.\n\nClear water filling or overflowing from one's home is one of the strongest auspicious dreams in the Korean tradition. It signals unexpected major fortune or incoming wealth, and practitioners of dream interpretation consistently identify it as one of the most welcome dreams a person can experience.",
        "imageRef": "auspicious"
      },
      {
        "heading": "When Clear Water Dreams Carry a Warning",
        "content": "Not every clear water dream brings unqualified good news. If calm, clear water suddenly begins forming waves or whirlpools, the dream shifts into cautionary territory. This pattern hints at potential conflict or disputes within the family or close circle. In this case, the turbulence of the waves matters more than the clarity of the water — an important distinction Korean dream interpretation emphasizes.\n\nFalling into clear water is a context-dependent dream. If the water was unmistakably clear and you felt at ease or able to swim after the fall, the dream reads as a neutral or even auspicious sign of new beginnings or needed life change. Some traditional interpretations actually read falling into clear water as a financial windfall. However, if the dreamer felt panic or fear during the fall, it may point to unexpected difficulties approaching — worth paying attention to in waking life.",
        "imageRef": "inauspicious"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Cultural and Historical Context",
        "content": "In Korean traditional dream interpretation, water stands as one of the most central symbols, representing wealth, power, and the very source of life. Clear water is sharply distinguished from murky water — the clarity of the water directly mirrors the brightness of one's fortune. This belief runs deep in Korea's ancient water deity worship, known as 수신신앙 (sushin sinang).\n\nIn the founding myth of Goguryeo, the mother of the founding ancestor Jumong was the daughter of Habaek, the river god. Water spirits were venerated as royal ancestors and national guardians. Fishing communities held elaborate abundance ceremonies for the dragon-king who ruled the seas, while farming communities performed rain-invocation rituals appealing to water deities during droughts. Water was understood not merely as a natural element but as a sacred conduit of abundance.\n\nWith the arrival of Buddhism in Korea, water gained an additional layer of meaning: purification and liberation from past karma. A clear-water dream thus came to carry a dual resonance — the promise of worldly fortune combined with the spiritual cleansing of past burdens. This is why clear water remains one of the most consistently positive dream symbols in Korean folk tradition to this day."
