    "culturalContext": "In Korean traditional dream interpretation, water stands as one of the most central symbols, representing wealth, power, and the source of life itself. Clear water in particular is sharply distinguished from murky water — the clarity of the water directly mirrors the brightness of one's fortune. The roots of this belief run deep in Korea's ancient water deity worship, known as 수신신앙 (sushin sinang). In the founding myth of Goguryeo, the mother of the progenitor Jumong was the daughter of Habaek, the river god — water spirits were venerated as royal ancestors and national guardians. Fishing communities held elaborate 풍어제 (abundance rituals) for the dragon-king who ruled the seas, while farming communities performed 기우제 (rain-invocation ceremonies) appealing to water deities during droughts. Water was thus understood not merely as a natural element but as a sacred conduit of abundance. Dreaming of clear water came to mean that this sacred energy had reached the dreamer, bringing blessings. With the arrival of Buddhism, water also gained the symbolic dimension of purification and liberation, adding the layer that a clear-water dream signals the washing away of past karma and the opening of a new chapter.",
    "westernContext": "Freud viewed water as a primal image linked to the unconscious, the origins of life, and the maternal principle. Seeing clear, clean water in a dream can be interpreted as a sign that repressed desires are being healthily expressed or that psychological conflicts are being resolved. Clean water may reflect an unconscious wish for rebirth — a desire to shed past burdens and return to a state of primal innocence.\n\nFor Jung, water is among the most powerful symbols of the collective unconscious, representing emotion, the depths of the psyche, and transformation as universal archetypes. Specifically, clear and transparent water suggests that the relationship between the ego and the unconscious is harmonious, and that the process of individuation is progressing well. Jung viewed vivid, intense water dreams as a signal that the individual stands at an important psychological turning point.\n\nModern psychology and neuroscience find that water dreams frequently emerge during REM sleep, when the brain is actively processing emotions and consolidating memories. Dreaming of clear water may reflect a currently stable emotional state with low levels of stress or internal conflict. Researchers suggest that the condition of water in dreams — clear vs. murky, calm vs. turbulent — tends to mirror the dreamer's present emotional state.\n\nWhile Korean traditional dream interpretation views clear water as a symbol of wealth luck and real-world good fortune, Western psychology reads it as a reflection of inner emotional clarity and the purification of the unconscious. Both traditions agree that clear water is a positive sign, but Korean interpretation focuses on external outcomes — wealth and success — while Western psychology emphasizes the inner world of emotions and the unconscious.",
