    "culturalContext": "In Korean folk tradition, water is the primary symbol of wealth, vitality, and life's flow — and a flood represents that force breaking free of all constraints. The Korean Encyclopedia of Folk Culture records over 700 place legends tied to flood mythology across the peninsula, revealing just how deeply embedded floods are in the Korean cultural imagination.\n\nIn Korean creation myths and sibling-union narratives (남매혼 설화), the great flood represents a cosmic reset: the dissolution of the old world order before a new one is established. This mythological background explains why flood dreams in Korea are not simply read as omens of disaster, but as signals of profound transformation.\n\nThe water's clarity is the critical interpretive key — clean, rushing water flowing into a home signals incoming wealth (재물 entering the household), while murky or red-tinged water warns of disruption, illness, or loss. Korea's history as an agrarian society dependent on rainfall and river management also gave floods enormous psychological weight: too little water meant famine, too much meant catastrophe, but the right flow meant abundance. This nuanced relationship with water explains why Korean dream interpretation around floods is far more sophisticated than a simple good/bad binary.",
    "westernContext": "In Freudian psychoanalysis, water represents the unconscious, and flooding symbolizes a 'drive overflow' — suppressed libidinal energy breaking through the ego's defenses and demanding expression. A powerful flood dream signals that repressed emotions or desires have reached a point where they can no longer be contained. The more destructive the flood, the more intense the underlying repression.\n\nFrom a Jungian perspective, water is the most universal symbol of the collective unconscious, and a flood represents the eruption of repressed unconscious contents — shadow material, archetypal energies — into conscious awareness. Jung viewed disaster dreams not as pure warnings but as part of the individuation process: an old ego structure must be inundated and destroyed so a deeper, more integrated Self can emerge. This framework aligns interestingly with the Korean tradition's view of floods as cosmic resets rather than mere calamities.\n\nModern cognitive psychology and neuroscience research suggest that flood dreams most commonly arise during periods of overwhelming stress, loss of control, or emotional overload that the waking mind struggles to process. During sleep, the brain re-processes unresolved emotional experiences from the day, and flooding is one of the most intuitive visual metaphors for being overwhelmed. Life transitions — job changes, moving, breakups — are also common triggers. Where Western psychology frames flood dreams primarily as signals of suppressed emotions erupting, Korean folk interpretation focuses on the water's clarity and direction of flow — reading clean, rushing water as an active portent of incoming wealth rather than merely a warning of internal crisis. Both frameworks, separated by thousands of miles, agree on one thing: the flood dream is rarely trivial.",
