    "culturalContext": "In Korean traditional culture, water is far more than a natural element — it is a sacred source of life deeply embedded in folk belief and shamanism. Water deities (수신) were revered across different water domains: ocean gods, river spirits, pond deities, and well spirits. Coastal communities built Dragon King shrines (용왕당) to pray for abundant catches and safe voyages. Sacred wells and springs were communal holy spaces, forming the tradition of 천수신앙 (spring water veneration). The founding myth of Goguryeo — where the kingdom's founder Jumong was born of the River God's daughter — illustrates how water symbolized royal legitimacy and the birth of great figures. After Buddhism arrived, water deity beliefs merged with protective Buddhism, further elevating water's sacred status. Rain-calling rituals (기우제) became important community ceremonies during drought. In this cultural landscape, dreaming of clear water is understood as a blessing flowing from divine favor and natural abundance.",
    "westernContext": "In Freudian psychoanalysis, water symbolizes the depths of the unconscious and suppressed emotional drives. Dreams of being submerged represent the ego's loosening grip as repressed wishes or impulses attempt to surface. Freud also connected water with the womb and the mother archetype, suggesting water dreams may relate to a longing to return to a primal state of birth or early security.\n\nFor Carl Jung, water is a central archetype of the collective unconscious. It represents the depths of the psyche — the vast inner world beyond conscious reach. Clear water symbolizes progress in individuation and integration with the Self, while murky or turbulent water may signal a confrontation with the Shadow — those darker aspects of the personality we resist acknowledging. From a Jungian perspective, water dreams reflect not just personal emotion but a universal human process of deep psychological transformation.\n\nModern psychology and neuroscience view water dreams as a product of the brain's emotional regulation system activating during sleep. People under high stress or anxiety tend to dream more frequently of drowning or being swept away by floods, which researchers interpret as the brain simulating and processing unresolved emotional pressures from waking life. Conversely, dreams featuring calm, clear water are often associated with lower stress levels and a more psychologically stable state.\n\nOne striking parallel: while Korean tradition reads water through a lens of wealth and divine blessing, and Western psychology views it as a symbol of the unconscious, both traditions converge on a shared principle — the clarity or murkiness of the water is the central factor in determining whether the dream carries a positive or negative meaning.",
