    "culturalContext": "In Korean tradition, fog has long been regarded as a symbol of invisibility and ambiguity. Historical records from the Three Kingdoms period through the Joseon Dynasty document fog under various names — Daemu (大霧, heavy fog), Hwangmu (黃霧, yellow fog), and Heungmu (黑霧, black fog) — classified by density, color, and time of occurrence. This suggests that ancient Koreans interpreted fog not merely as a meteorological event but as a heavenly omen or sign of the times.\n\nIn the dream interpretation tradition, fog primarily symbolizes an unclear future or a situation where truth is obscured — reflecting fog's natural property of blocking vision and causing one to lose one's way. The Korean proverb 'Dense morning fog heralds clear skies' also influenced dream interpretation: because thick fog naturally gives way to brilliant sunshine, dreams in which fog clears became understood as auspicious signs of resolution and clarity.\n\nIn Korean shamanism (무속신앙), fog and clouds were also seen as veils concealing the realm of spirits and mountain deities. Mountain fog in particular carried a sacred, mystical quality — the presence of divine beings hiding within the mist. This layered cultural understanding gives fog in Korean dreams a richness that goes well beyond simple weather symbolism: it bridges the visible and invisible worlds, the known and the unknown.",
    "westernContext": "From a Freudian psychoanalytic perspective, fog symbolizes repressed emotions or hidden desires that linger in the unconscious without fully breaking through to conscious awareness. The vision-obscuring quality of fog can represent ego defense mechanisms that prevent one from confronting uncomfortable truths or inner conflicts. Freud connected fog imagery to decisiveness and mental clarity, suggesting such dreams may reflect psychological indecisiveness or a tendency toward avoidance of difficult realities.\n\nIn Jung's analytical psychology, fog symbolizes the threshold between the conscious and unconscious mind — the domain of psychic material not yet integrated by the ego. Fog serves as both a gateway to the collective unconscious and a representation of the shadow, anima, or animus that remains unrecognized. Jung would interpret the clearing of fog as the process of individuation — integrating unconscious material into consciousness and moving toward self-realization. In this reading, a fog dream is not a warning but an invitation to look inward.\n\nFrom a modern cognitive psychology and neuroscience perspective, fog dreams are understood as the brain's way of processing anxiety, stress, or emotional turmoil that accompanies significant life decisions or transitions. During sleep, reduced frontal lobe activity allows the limbic system — responsible for emotion and memory — to generate vivid but unclear imagery. Research indicates that individuals with anxiety or depression tend to report dreams involving obscured vision or disorientation more frequently.\n\nWhere Korean traditional dream interpretation links fog to specific external events — disasters, hidden secrets, or the resolution of troubles — Western psychology treats fog as a mirror of inner emotional states. The two perspectives together offer a fuller picture: fog dreams may be both a practical nudge to examine external circumstances and an invitation to do the internal work of clarifying what you truly want and where you are truly headed.",
