    "culturalContext": "In Korean tradition, rain is far more than weather — it has long been viewed as a sacred medium connecting heaven and humanity. In an agrarian society where rain determined the difference between harvest and famine, rain was revered as a symbol of wealth and prosperity. This belief crystallized in the 기우제 (giu-je), a traditional rain-prayer ritual in which communities and the royal court would offer ceremonies to the dragon deity (용신, yongsin) or mountain spirit to invoke rainfall. The dragon's role as master of water explains why rain dreams involving dragon imagery are considered among the most auspicious possible. In Korean shamanism (무속, musok), water and rain are treated as purifying and healing forces, and shamans performing rituals would sprinkle water to cleanse malevolent energy. This dual nature of rain — simultaneously washing away impurity and nourishing growth — explains the breadth of rain dream interpretations: heavy, welcome rain fills and enriches, while violent or endless rain overwhelms and destroys.",
    "westernContext": "In Freudian psychoanalysis, rain represents the release of repressed emotions. Rain in a dream is interpreted as the unconscious discharge of sadness, desire, or emotional tension that has been suppressed in waking life. Being soaked by rain in particular may suggest that pent-up inner drives are finally overwhelming the ego's defenses — not necessarily a bad thing, since emotional release often precedes clarity.\n\nJungian analytical psychology treats rain as an archetypal symbol linked to the collective unconscious. Just as water symbolizes the unconscious itself, rain falling from sky to earth represents the moment consciousness meets the unconscious — a process of psychological transformation and individuation. A dream of clearing skies after rain can be interpreted as successful shadow integration and the restoration of inner balance. In this reading, the dreamer who weathers the rain and sees it clear has integrated something difficult into their psyche.\n\nFrom a modern cognitive neuroscience perspective, rain dreams arise as the brain processes emotional memories and stress during REM sleep. Weather imagery like rain tends to appear when the dreamer is experiencing change, uncertainty, or emotional overload in waking life. Recurring rain dreams may be the brain's signal that unresolved conflict or suppressed emotion needs attention.\n\nKorean folk interpretation reads rain primarily as an external omen of wealth and purification, while Western psychology frames rain as a reflection of the dreamer's internal emotional state and unconscious processes — two traditions that differ in focus but both recognize rain as a symbol of transformation.",
