    "culturalContext": "In Korean dream interpretation, wind-related dreams have long been understood as 'heralds of change.' The cultural weight of wind in Korea traces back to the Dangun creation myth, where Pungbaek (풍백 — the Wind God) is the very first divine attendant named when the heavenly prince Hwanung descends to earth. This places wind at the foundation of Korean cosmology, marking it as a sacred force rather than a mere natural phenomenon. The whirlwind (회오리바람) traditionally symbolized unexpected calamity and trials due to its unpredictable, destructive nature. In Korean shamanic belief (무속 신앙), powerful whirlwinds could signal spirits or malevolent forces in motion. At the same time, a whirlwind sweeping things away carried the meaning of clearing out the old to make room for the new — a destructive-but-cleansing energy. Because tornadoes are not native to Korea's geography, classical Korean dream texts worked primarily with the whirlwind (旋風/회오리). In modern Korean dream culture, however, the Western tornado concept has merged with this traditional symbol, intensifying its associations with overwhelming, life-altering transformation.",
    "westernContext": "From a Freudian perspective, tornado dreams represent suppressed emotions — particularly intense drives like anger, anxiety, or libidinal energy — erupting from the unconscious mind. The tornado's swirling, overpowering form symbolizes psychic energy that the waking mind has been holding down. Freud would read this dream as a signal that something long-suppressed has reached a breaking point: it can no longer be contained by conscious control alone.\n\nJungian analytical psychology sees the tornado as an archetype of wild, untamed psychic energy — often representing the Shadow, the unconscious aspects of the self we tend to deny or push aside. During the individuation process, a tornado dream sends a powerful message from the unconscious: there is something you must face directly. The tornado also carries the archetypal energy of destruction-as-renewal — the old self is being dismantled so that a more mature, integrated identity can emerge in its place.\n\nModern psychology and cognitive neuroscience frame tornado dreams more pragmatically: they are the brain's way of processing intense stress, anxiety, or major life transitions such as career changes, relocation, or relationship endings. During sleep, the brain consolidates unprocessed emotional experiences, and the stronger the waking sense of helplessness or loss of control, the more dramatically destructive the dream imagery tends to become. People who have experienced trauma sometimes have recurring tornado dreams — these are understood as part of the brain's emotional integration and healing process.\n\nWhat is striking is how closely Korean traditional interpretation and Western psychology converge on the core themes. Korean folk wisdom sees the tornado as an omen of trials and imminent change; Western psychology sees it as a mirror of the dreamer's inner world. Both traditions, from completely different starting points, arrive at the same essential insight: this dream is about radical transformation — and whether it destroys you or makes you stronger is a question only the dreamer can answer.",
