    "culturalContext": "In Korean traditional culture, trees were revered not merely as plants but as cosmic pillars connecting heaven and earth. The founding myth of Korea — in which the divine Hwanung descended to earth beneath the sacred altar tree (신단수) on Mount Taebaek — established the tree as a holy meeting place between gods and humans. This wasn't just mythology: throughout Korean history, every village maintained a 당산나무 (village guardian tree) and 서낭나무 (shrine tree), which served as sites for communal ritual, prayer, and spiritual communication.\n\nWithin this cultural framework, a fruit-bearing tree appearing in a dream carries meaning far beyond simple good luck. It signals that the generative power of nature and cosmic abundance is flowing toward the dreamer. Because fruit represents the full completion of a natural cycle — from planting seed, through growth, to harvest — traditional Korean dream interpretation treats it as a powerful auspicious symbol encompassing wealth, offspring, personal achievement, and even the precious gift of new life in pregnancy dreams. To dream of such a tree is to receive what Korean tradition might call a message from heaven: your patience and effort have been seen, and the reward is coming.",
    "westernContext": "Western psychology offers a rich set of complementary lenses for interpreting fruit tree dreams — one that differs fascinatingly from the Korean folk tradition while ultimately arriving at a similar core message.\n\nFrom a Freudian psychoanalytic perspective, the tree represents the mother and the maternal principle, while fruit symbolizes wish fulfillment and the satisfaction of unfulfilled desires. The act of picking fruit from a tree may reflect an unconscious drive to actively realize suppressed longings. Abundant fruit can be linked to libidinal energy at full expression, or a deep psychological hunger for nourishment, abundance, and care — themes that translate naturally into the waking desire for financial security and emotional fulfillment.\n\nJungian analytical psychology reads the tree as one of the most powerful archetypal symbols of the Self — the center of the psyche that integrates conscious and unconscious experience through the individuation process. A fruit-bearing tree, in this reading, is a profound image from the collective unconscious: it signals that the dreamer is approaching psychological wholeness, that inner maturity is bearing fruit. This is a symbol that appears across world mythology — the Tree of Life, the World Tree, the Garden of Eden — always carrying the same charge of completion and realized potential.\n\nModern cognitive psychology and neuroscience take a more grounded approach: dreaming of a fruit-bearing tree most often reflects the brain's processing of anticipation around goal achievement and the psychological desire to see one's efforts rewarded. These dreams tend to cluster around life transitions — before major exams, when a business is approaching a milestone, during pregnancy. The brain consolidates memories and emotions related to achievement and reward during sleep, and the fruit tree becomes the mind's natural metaphor for that consolidation.\n\nWhat's striking is the convergence: whether read through Korean folk tradition, Freudian symbolism, Jungian archetypes, or cognitive psychology, the fruit-bearing tree consistently points toward a moment of completion. The harvest is coming — the question is simply what language you use to describe it.",
