    "culturalContext": "In Korean folk tradition, flowers have long represented life's most auspicious moments — beauty, vitality, and the transient preciousness of good fortune. Flowers were considered the highest offering to the divine in both shamanic and Buddhist practices, serving as symbols of purification and the invitation of blessings into the home and community.\n\nDuring the Goryeo period, the lotus flower was venerated as an emblem of the Buddhist Pure Land; peonies stood for prosperity and nobility; plum blossoms represented scholarly integrity; chrysanthemums symbolized unwavering uprightness. Each flower carried a specific cultural weight that has filtered into dream interpretation.\n\nHistorical records from the Joseon era describe a striking anecdote: candidates for the state civil examination came to a dream interpreter worried that they had each dreamed of flowers falling from their branches — an image they feared was inauspicious. The interpreter reassured them: 'When flowers fall, fruit must follow.' All three men passed their examinations. This story, preserved across centuries, captures the deep-rooted Korean instinct to read flower dreams as omens of earned success and recognition.\n\nThe act of receiving flowers is naturally linked in Korean culture to life's most celebratory moments — graduations, weddings, award ceremonies. Dreaming of being given flowers draws on this rich cultural backdrop to signal incoming love, recognition, and good fortune bestowed by others.",
