    "culturalContext": "The lotus occupies an exceptionally rich symbolic space in Korean culture, weaving together Buddhist, Confucian, and folk traditions across more than a millennium. In Buddhism — historically the dominant religion on the Korean peninsula — the lotus is the supreme emblem of purity and spiritual transcendence: it roots in muddy pond floors yet blooms immaculate above the water, perfectly expressing the practitioner's aspiration to remain unstained by worldly defilement. Buddhist paradise itself is called the 'Lotus Treasury World' (연화장세계), and the lotus throne appears beneath every major deity in temple iconography. In the Confucian tradition that shaped Korean court and scholarly culture, the Song-dynasty philosopher Zhou Maoshu famously called the lotus 'the gentleman among flowers' because it 'emerges from mud unstained' — the perfect metaphor for the incorruptible scholar-official. This ideal permeated Joseon dynasty literati culture, making lotus motifs ubiquitous in folk paintings and celadon ceramics. In folk custom, the lotus's prolific seed production made it a fertility talisman: lotus patterns were embroidered on women's garments as prayers for many children and prosperous descendants. This layered cultural heritage means that in Korean dream interpretation, the lotus is almost universally a powerful auspicious sign, symbolizing honor, wealth, blessed children, and spiritual elevation.",
