    "culturalContext": "In Korean traditional culture, flowers were more than decoration — they were a living language of auspicious events. The expression '그 집에 꽃이 폈다' ('a flower has bloomed in that household') was used to announce weddings, births, and remarkable achievements, and '꽃 같은 시절' ('flower-like years') described the prime of one's life. These expressions persist in everyday Korean today.\n\nThe Joseon-era scholar Kang Hee-an famously ranked flowers not by beauty but by moral virtue, elevating plum blossoms, chrysanthemums, the lotus, and bamboo as symbols of ethical integrity. This reveals how deeply flowers were connected to character and social standing — not just aesthetics — in Korean thought.\n\nIn Korean shamanism (무속 신앙), flowers served as sacred offerings to divine spirits and were used to adorn altar spaces during ritual ceremonies known as 굿. The Buddhist tradition gave the lotus particular prominence as a symbol of spiritual purity — rising unstained from muddy water — making it central to temple offerings and contemplative practice across the peninsula.\n\nThe traditional association between flower-blooming dreams and daughters connects to the long-held cultural linkage between flowers and femininity — delicacy, beauty, and nurturing grace. As an agrarian society, Koreans also naturally associated seasonal bloom with abundant harvest, making the arrival of flowers a culturally embedded shorthand for prosperity and good fortune.",
