    "culturalContext": "In Korean traditional culture, flowers carried layered symbolic weight far beyond their natural beauty. In Buddhism, the lotus emerging from muddy water embodied purity and enlightenment, appearing prominently in temple paintings and mandalas. Under Confucian influence, chrysanthemums and plum blossoms became emblems of scholarly integrity and noble character, forming part of the Four Gentlemen (사군자) in literati painting. Within shamanic tradition (무속 신앙), specific flowers were used in rituals to summon or appease ancestor spirits, and peach blossoms were employed as talismans against evil spirits. The native azalea (진달래) became the flower of common people, deeply embedded in folk songs and literature as a symbol of longing and farewell — most famously in Kim Sowol's poem 'Azalea Flowers.' These cultural layers shaped flower dream interpretation: flowers generally signal auspiciousness, honor, love, and wealth, while the condition of the flower — blooming, falling, or withered — is the primary factor determining whether a dream is fortunate or ominous.",
    "westernContext": "Western psychology offers a fascinating counterpoint to Korean flower dream interpretation — one that shifts the gaze inward rather than outward toward the future.\n\nFreudian psychoanalysis reads flowers as archetypal symbols of femininity and sexuality. The open, receptive form of a flower represents the female body, and actions such as picking or receiving flowers are interpreted as unconscious expressions of sexual desire or libidinal energy. From this perspective, even a dream of tending a flowerpot may be understood as the emergence of repressed wishes for intimacy or creative expression.\n\nCarl Jung's analytical psychology brings a different lens. Flowers can manifest as archetypal images of the Self — their radial, symmetrical structure echoing the mandala, which Jung associated with psychological wholeness and integration. Receiving flowers or walking through a flower field in a dream may represent an encounter with the anima archetype — the inner feminine energy — in the ongoing process of individuation. Flowers blooming against harsh conditions, like winter blooms, reflect the collective unconscious motif of regeneration and the endurance of hope.\n\nModern neuroscience and psychology offer yet another layer: flower dreams may simply reflect the dreamer's current emotional state and needs. A dream of flowers bursting into bloom may signal that stress is lifting and emotional reward circuits are activating. Wilting or falling flowers, by contrast, may represent the mind's ongoing work of processing grief, loss, or unresolved sadness during REM sleep.\n\nWhat makes flower dreams so cross-culturally rich is this: both Korean 꿈해몽 and Western psychology agree that flowers connect to beauty, flourishing, and the feminine — yet they diverge in their orientation. Korean tradition treats the flower as a window into future reality; Western psychology treats it as a mirror of present internal experience. Neither interpretation is more 'correc