    "culturalContext": "In traditional Korean culture, trees held a sacred status that went far beyond their botanical nature. Almost every traditional village maintained a sacred tree called the dangsan-namu (당산나무, village guardian tree), wrapped in twisted straw rope and white paper to mark its divine status. Regular communal rituals called dangje (당제) were performed at these trees to pray for good harvests, community health, and protection from misfortune. It was widely believed that harming or cutting a village guardian tree would bring calamity to the entire community.\n\nIn Korean shamanism (무속신앙), trees served as living conduits through which mountain spirits (산신) and wood spirits (목신) descended to interact with the human world. This deep spiritual association explains why trees in dreams carry such direct forecasting power — a flourishing tree signals divine blessing and life force, while a fallen tree signals the withdrawal of spiritual protection.\n\nWithin the traditional Korean Five Elements (오행) framework, wood (木) governs spring, the east, and the energy of growth and new beginnings. This elemental significance adds another layer to tree dream interpretation, connecting it to concepts of seasonal renewal and directional fortune in Korean traditional cosmology.",
    "westernContext": "Western psychology offers a rich, multi-layered view of tree dreams that complements and deepens the Korean traditional interpretation in fascinating ways.\n\nFrom a Freudian psychoanalytic perspective, the tree trunk is a classic phallic symbol representing male authority, physical power, and social standing. Cutting or breaking a tree may express unconscious aggression toward a dominant authority figure, or symbolize castration anxiety in the context of power dynamics. Climbing a tree connects to achievement drives and ambition, while picking fruit can be interpreted as wish fulfillment around sexuality or reward.\n\nJungian analytical psychology treats the tree as one of the most important archetypes in the human psyche — a symbol of the Self and the individuation process. The roots represent the unconscious; the trunk, the ego; the branches, the choices available in life; the leaves, the full flowering of personal identity. As the axis mundi (World Tree), it integrates opposites: heaven and earth, conscious and unconscious. Jung famously described the tree as 'the most natural image of the soul's growth,' and a healthy, flourishing tree in a dream signals genuine progress toward psychological wholeness and integration.\n\nFrom modern cognitive psychology and neuroscience, tree dreams tend to emerge during periods of personal transition — career shifts, relocations, relationship changes. The brain uses the deep-rooted stability of a tree as a symbolic stand-in for the dreamer's own sense of groundedness or instability. People who report frequent tree dreams are often unconsciously asking themselves: 'How firmly am I rooted? In which direction am I growing?'\n\nThe convergence is striking: Korean tradition reads the tree's condition as an external forecast of fortune and family health, while Western psychology reads it as an internal mirror of psychological stability and growth. Both traditions agree, however, that the tree is one of the most powerful symbolic languages the dreaming mind uses to communicate about life force, development, and the deepest human drive to flourish.",
